## REGISTERS OF THE STRING PATH. `addvs` (and the helpers it calls) uses %r12, %r13 and %r14 as scratch and does not
## save them. That is the convention of this runtime, and it is safe because nothing that can reach `addv` holds a
## value in a callee-saved register across the call:
##   * code generated by lumec keeps every live value in a stack slot (05_codegen never allocates %rbx, %r12..%r15
##     to a Lume value), and `addv` is called from generated code only (lbin_add / lbin_rt; no runtime routine calls it);
##   * a C caller of an exported function gets %rbx, %r12..%r15 back from the export stub (lffi_export_stub);
##   * an interrupted context gets them back from the interrupt stub (`__isr_hw_32` in runtime_sys_tail saves and
##     restores %rbx, %r12..%r15 around the handler; it used to save the caller-saved registers only, which was the
##     one way a routine in the middle of using %r12..%r14 could lose them).
fn rt_add() = ""

