## Mutable byte buffers: a cell [(len<<8)|15][len bytes], allocated atomic (bytes only, never scanned) and changed in
## place, so every holder of a buffer sees buf_set / buf_copy. Every access is bounds-checked; a bad one stops the
## program (or fails the enclosing `try`) like a bad string index. buf_from_str / buf_to_str copy between a buffer
## and a str; a str carries every byte 0..255 (its length is in its cell), so files and sockets move buffers as str.
fn rt_buf() = ""

## f64: IEEE 754 doubles in kind-17 cells (header 8 << 8 | 17, the bits at +8, atomic). + - * / and comparisons
## reach them through the slow paths of addv, __rt_sub, __rt_mul, __rt_div, cmpv and eqv (an int operand is converted);
## str/print give the shortest text that reads back to the same double (x87 at 64-bit precision, the control word
## set and restored around every use, so Linux and Windows print the same digits). Only xmm0-xmm2 are used (the
## Win64 ABI keeps xmm6-xmm15).
fn rt_f64() = rt_lume_f64()

## a program that calls no f64 builtin can hold no f64 cell: the hooks of addv, __rt_sub, __rt_mul, __rt_div, cmpv,
## eqv, str and print find .Lrt_anyf = 0 and never reach the other labels. This keeps such programs (the toolchain
## among them, assembled by the seed at stage 0) free of SSE and x87 instructions.
fn rt_f64_stub() = "__rt_anyf:\n" + ".Lrt_anyf:\n" + "    xorq %rax, %rax\n" + "    ret\n" + "__rt_fadd:\n" + ".Lrt_fadd:\n" + "__rt_fsub:\n" + ".Lrt_fsub:\n" + "__rt_fmul:\n" + ".Lrt_fmul:\n" + "__rt_fdiv:\n" + ".Lrt_fdiv:\n" + "__rt_fcmp:\n" + ".Lrt_fcmp:\n" + "__rt_eqvf:\n" + ".Lrt_eqvf:\n" + "__rt_fstr:\n" + "    jmp eqv0\n"

